-- Safety I — Hazards, Risk Assessment & Approach Boundaries
insert into public.roadmap_modules (
  id, phase, phase_title, order_in_phase, title, description, domain, neta_reference,
  estimated_minutes, total_questions, content_sections, is_published, passing_score,
  neta_level, video_recommended
) values (
  '19247004-7cb2-4f6b-bb3c-16bde4e6ff7e', 1, 'Foundation', 2, $sfty$Safety I — Hazards, Risk Assessment & Approach Boundaries$sfty$, $sfty$Who writes the safety rules, how to assess electrical risk, qualified vs. unqualified persons, shock approach boundaries, when energized work is allowed, and confined space basics.$sfty$,
  'Safety', $sfty$NFPA 70E Art. 105, 110, 130.2, 130.4; OSHA 29 CFR 1910 Subpart S, 1910.146; NETA L2 Outline Safety A, F, I$sfty$, 30, 10,
  $sfty${"overview": "Safety is 15% of the NETA Level 2 exam, and every field task starts with it. This module covers the foundation: which codes and standards govern electrical safety and which one is actually law, how a risk assessment works, what makes someone a qualified person, and the shock approach boundaries you must know by voltage class. It also covers when energized work is allowed at all, and how to recognize a permit-required confined space like a vault or manhole. Section references are to NFPA 70E (2018 edition); later editions renumber a few tables, but the concepts and values tested here are the same.", "sections": [{"title": "Who Writes the Rules — OSHA, NFPA, NEC and ANSI/NETA", "content": "Several organizations shape how you work, and the exam expects you to know what each one does. OSHA regulations (29 CFR 1910 for general industry, including Subpart S for electrical work) are federal law and are enforceable. NFPA 70E, Standard for Electrical Safety in the Workplace, is a consensus standard: it is not law by itself, but it is the recognized way to meet OSHA's general requirements, so employers and courts treat it as the benchmark. NFPA 70 (the National Electrical Code) covers how equipment is installed, while NFPA 70B covers maintenance. ANSI/NETA ATS, MTS and ECS define what to test and the acceptance criteria. ASTM writes the test and performance specifications for protective equipment (for example rubber gloves), IEEE 1584 is the guide for arc flash calculations, NEMA writes equipment standards, and in Canada CSA Z462 plays the role of NFPA 70E. On top of all of these, the manufacturer's published instructions and labeling apply to the specific equipment in front of you — when they are stricter, they govern.", "key_points": ["OSHA 29 CFR 1910 is enforceable law; NFPA 70E is the consensus standard used to comply with it", "NEC (NFPA 70) = installation; NFPA 70B = maintenance; NFPA 70E = safe work practices", "ASTM = PPE test specs; IEEE 1584 = arc flash calculations; CSA Z462 = Canada's version of 70E", "Manufacturer's instructions and labels apply to the specific equipment and must be followed"], "standards_reference": "NFPA 70E Art. 90, 105; OSHA 29 CFR 1910.331–.335; NETA L2 Outline Safety I"}, {"title": "Risk Assessment and the Hierarchy of Controls", "content": "Before any task, NFPA 70E requires a risk assessment: identify the electrical hazards (shock, arc flash, arc blast), estimate how likely an injury is and how severe it could be, and decide what protective measures are needed. The standard ranks risk controls in a hierarchy, from most to least effective: elimination (put the equipment in an electrically safe work condition), substitution, engineering controls, awareness (signs, barricades, labels), administrative controls (procedures, training, permits), and finally PPE. PPE is the last line of defense, not the first — it only reduces injury when everything above it has failed. Every job also starts with a job briefing that covers the hazards, the procedures, special precautions, energy source controls, and the PPE requirements. If conditions change during the job, you brief again. Human error is expected, so the risk assessment has to account for it rather than assume everyone performs perfectly.", "key_points": ["Risk assessment = identify hazards, estimate likelihood and severity, choose protective measures", "Hierarchy of controls: elimination → substitution → engineering → awareness → administrative → PPE", "De-energizing (elimination) is the most effective control; PPE is the least effective", "Hold a job briefing before each job, and again when the scope or conditions change"], "standards_reference": "NFPA 70E 110.1(G)–(I), 130.3; NETA L2 Outline Safety A.1–A.2"}, {"title": "Qualified vs. Unqualified Persons and Training", "content": "A qualified person has been trained on the construction and operation of the specific equipment or work method and can identify and avoid its electrical hazards. Qualification is task-specific: you can be qualified on low-voltage panelboards and still be unqualified on 15 kV switchgear. To work inside the limited approach boundary, a qualified person must also be able to tell exposed energized parts from other parts, determine nominal voltage, know the approach distances for the voltages involved, and make the job-planning decisions (hazard identification, risk assessment, choosing controls). A trainee working under the direct supervision of a qualified person is treated as qualified for the specific duties they have shown they can do safely. Employees must be trained to select and use a test instrument to verify absence of voltage. The employer checks compliance through supervision or inspections at least annually, retraining happens at intervals not exceeding 3 years (or sooner if work practices slip, equipment or procedures change, or duties change), and training is documented and kept for the length of employment.", "key_points": ["Qualification is equipment- and task-specific, not a blanket title", "Trainees under direct supervision of a qualified person may perform the tasks they've been trained on", "Employer verifies compliance at least annually; retraining at least every 3 years", "Training records include content, employee name and dates, and are kept for the duration of employment"], "standards_reference": "NFPA 70E 110.2(A); NETA L2 Outline Safety A"}, {"title": "Shock Approach Boundaries", "content": "Shock protection uses two boundaries measured from the exposed energized part. The limited approach boundary is the line unqualified persons may not cross unless a qualified person escorts them and advises them of the hazard. The restricted approach boundary is much closer: only qualified persons may cross it, and only with shock-protective PPE (rubber gloves and more) and a plan that keeps any part of the body from contacting energized parts. Both come from tables by nominal voltage and do not depend on fault current. For AC systems with fixed circuit parts: 50–150 V has a limited approach boundary of 3 ft 6 in and a restricted boundary of 'avoid contact'; 151–750 V is 3 ft 6 in limited and 1 ft 0 in restricted; 751 V–15 kV is 5 ft 0 in limited and 2 ft 2 in restricted; 15.1–36 kV is 6 ft 0 in and 2 ft 9 in. For movable conductors like overhead lines, the limited approach boundary is 10 ft up through 72.5 kV. Do not confuse these with the arc flash boundary, which comes from incident energy (1.2 cal/cm²) and is covered in Safety III.", "key_points": ["Limited approach boundary: unqualified persons stay out unless escorted by a qualified person", "Restricted approach boundary: qualified persons only, with shock PPE and a documented plan", "480 V (151–750 V class): limited 3 ft 6 in, restricted 1 ft 0 in", "Shock boundaries depend on voltage only; the arc flash boundary depends on incident energy"], "standards_reference": "NFPA 70E 130.4 and Table 130.4(D)(a)/(b) (2018 numbering); NETA L2 Outline Safety A.3, D.1"}, {"title": "When Energized Work Is Allowed", "content": "The default is simple: conductors and circuit parts at 50 V or more must be put in an electrically safe work condition before work when you are inside the limited approach boundary, or when you interact with equipment in a way that increases the likelihood of an arc flash injury. Energized work is only justified in two cases: de-energizing would create a greater hazard (life support, emergency alarms, hazardous-location ventilation), or the task is infeasible de-energized because of equipment design or operations — which is why diagnostics, testing and troubleshooting are often done live. Justified energized work inside the restricted approach boundary needs an energized electrical work permit that documents the equipment, the justification, the shock and arc flash risk assessment results, the PPE, how unqualified persons are kept out, the job briefing, and management approval. Testing, troubleshooting and voltage measuring by qualified persons using the right PPE are exempt from the permit — but not from the boundaries and PPE. Normal operation of equipment (such as operating a breaker) is allowed only when it is properly installed and maintained, doors are closed and secured, covers are in place, and there is no evidence of impending failure.", "key_points": ["≥50 V inside the limited approach boundary → electrically safe work condition is the default", "Energized work only for greater hazard or infeasibility — never for convenience or schedule", "Energized electrical work permit is required inside the restricted approach boundary", "Testing, troubleshooting and voltage measuring are permit-exempt but still need boundaries and PPE"], "standards_reference": "NFPA 70E 130.2, 130.2(A)–(B); NETA L2 Outline Safety A.1–A.2"}, {"title": "Confined Spaces — Vaults, Manholes and Tanks", "content": "Electrical techs regularly work in transformer vaults, manholes and cable tunnels, which are often confined spaces under OSHA 29 CFR 1910.146. A confined space is large enough to enter and do work, has limited or restricted means of entry or exit, and is not designed for continuous occupancy. It becomes a permit-required confined space if it has one or more serious hazards: a hazardous atmosphere (or the potential for one), a material that could engulf the entrant, an internal shape that could trap or asphyxiate, or any other serious safety hazard — including exposed energized conductors. Permit entry needs defined roles: authorized entrants, an attendant who stays outside and monitors (and never enters to attempt a rescue), and an entry supervisor who signs the permit. Test the atmosphere before entry and monitor it during the work, in this order: oxygen first (acceptable range 19.5% to 23.5%), then flammable gases (below 10% of the lower explosive limit), then toxic gases.", "key_points": ["Confined space = enterable, limited entry/exit, not designed for continuous occupancy", "Permit-required if it has atmospheric, engulfment, entrapment or other serious hazards (including electrical)", "Roles: entrant, attendant (stays outside, never enters for rescue), entry supervisor", "Test atmosphere in order: oxygen (19.5–23.5%), flammables (<10% LEL), toxics"], "standards_reference": "OSHA 29 CFR 1910.146; NETA L2 Outline Safety F"}], "key_values": [{"label": "Voltage threshold for an electrically safe work condition", "value": "50 V", "context": "At 50 V and above, work inside the limited approach boundary requires an electrically safe work condition unless energized work is justified (NFPA 70E 130.2)."}, {"label": "Limited approach boundary, 151–750 V AC (fixed parts)", "value": "3 ft 6 in (1.0 m)", "context": "Covers 208 V, 480 V and 600 V systems. Unqualified persons stay outside unless escorted."}, {"label": "Restricted approach boundary, 151–750 V AC", "value": "1 ft 0 in (0.3 m)", "context": "Only qualified persons with shock PPE and a plan may cross it. For 50–150 V the restricted boundary is 'avoid contact'."}, {"label": "Approach boundaries, 751 V–15 kV AC (fixed parts)", "value": "Limited 5 ft 0 in / Restricted 2 ft 2 in", "context": "Covers typical medium-voltage distribution (4.16 kV, 13.8 kV)."}, {"label": "Limited approach boundary for movable conductors", "value": "10 ft 0 in (3.0 m) up to 72.5 kV", "context": "Applies where the distance to the conductor isn't under the person's control, such as overhead lines."}, {"label": "Electrical safety retraining interval", "value": "Not to exceed 3 years", "context": "Sooner if audits show non-compliance, equipment or procedures change, or job duties change (NFPA 70E 110.2(A)(3))."}, {"label": "Employee compliance check", "value": "At least annually", "context": "The employer verifies through supervision or inspections that each employee follows safe work practices."}, {"label": "Safe oxygen range for confined space entry", "value": "19.5% to 23.5%", "context": "Test oxygen first, then flammables (<10% LEL), then toxics (OSHA 1910.146)."}], "exam_tips": ["If a question asks which document is legally enforceable, the answer is OSHA. NFPA 70E is the consensus standard that tells you how to comply.", "Shock boundaries come from voltage alone; the arc flash boundary comes from incident energy. A question that gives you fault current and clearing time is asking about arc flash, not shock.", "Know 480 V cold: limited approach 3 ft 6 in, restricted approach 1 ft 0 in. For 120 V the restricted boundary is 'avoid contact'.", "'It's faster' or 'the customer doesn't want an outage' is never a valid reason for energized work. Only greater hazard or infeasibility qualify.", "Testing, troubleshooting and voltage measurement are exempt from the energized work permit, but you still need PPE and must respect the boundaries — exam distractors often say they're exempt from PPE.", "In confined space questions, the attendant never enters to rescue. The first atmospheric test is always oxygen."], "summary": "OSHA is the law; NFPA 70E is how you meet it, with the NEC, 70B, ANSI/NETA, ASTM and IEEE covering installation, maintenance, testing, PPE specs and arc flash calculations. Every task starts with a risk assessment that follows the hierarchy of controls, where de-energizing is best and PPE is last. Qualification is task-specific, verified at least annually and refreshed at least every 3 years. Shock boundaries depend on voltage — at 480 V, limited is 3 ft 6 in and restricted is 1 ft. At 50 V and above, the default is an electrically safe work condition; energized work needs a real justification and, inside the restricted boundary, a permit. Vaults and manholes are often permit-required confined spaces: test oxygen first and keep the attendant outside."}$sfty$::jsonb, true, 80, 2, false
)
