-- Safety II — Electrically Safe Work Condition, LOTO & Grounding
insert into public.roadmap_modules (
  id, phase, phase_title, order_in_phase, title, description, domain, neta_reference,
  estimated_minutes, total_questions, content_sections, is_published, passing_score,
  neta_level, video_recommended
) values (
  'b7e2c1a4-5d3f-4e8a-9c61-2f0d8a4b7e11', 1, 'Foundation', 3, $sfty$Safety II — Electrically Safe Work Condition, LOTO & Grounding$sfty$, $sfty$The steps to establish an electrically safe work condition, lockout/tagout rules, verifying absence of voltage with live-dead-live, finding every isolation point, and applying temporary protective grounds.$sfty$,
  'Safety', $sfty$NFPA 70E Art. 120 (120.2, 120.5), 110.2(B); OSHA 29 CFR 1910.147, 1910.333; ASTM F855; NETA L2 Outline Safety B, C, G$sfty$, 30, 10,
  $sfty${"overview": "An electrically safe work condition (ESWC) is the single most effective way to protect yourself, and building one is a procedure, not a feeling. This module walks through the eight steps NFPA 70E requires, the lockout/tagout rules that make them stick, how to verify absence of voltage correctly, how to find every source that could re-energize your work, and when and how to apply temporary protective grounds. Until every step is done, the equipment is treated as energized — which means the verification itself is done in PPE.", "sections": [{"title": "What an Electrically Safe Work Condition Is", "content": "An electrically safe work condition is a state where the conductor or circuit part has been disconnected from all energized sources, its disconnects are locked and tagged, it has been tested to verify the absence of voltage, and — where needed — it has been temporarily grounded. It is not established by opening a breaker, and it is not established by reading a one-line diagram. The key rule for the exam and the field: until every step is complete, the equipment must be treated as energized. That's why the person testing for absence of voltage wears the PPE required for the shock and arc flash hazard of that equipment; the test is energized work, because you don't yet know it's dead. Each qualified person working on the equipment must be protected by the lockout, and the equipment stays in an ESWC only as long as those controls stay in place.", "key_points": ["ESWC = disconnected, locked/tagged, tested for absence of voltage, and grounded where necessary", "Equipment is considered energized until the ESWC is fully established", "Verifying absence of voltage is energized work — wear the required PPE while testing", "Opening a breaker alone never creates an electrically safe work condition"], "standards_reference": "NFPA 70E Art. 100 (definition), 120.5; NETA L2 Outline Safety B"}, {"title": "The Eight Steps, in Order", "content": "NFPA 70E lists the steps to establish and verify an ESWC, performed in this order where feasible. (1) Determine all possible sources of supply using up-to-date drawings, diagrams and identification tags. (2) After properly interrupting the load current, open the disconnecting device for each source — don't use a disconnect switch to break load it isn't rated to interrupt. (3) Where possible, visually verify that all blades of the disconnect are fully open, or that drawout breakers are withdrawn to the fully disconnected position. (4) Release stored electrical energy, such as capacitors. (5) Release or block stored mechanical energy, such as charged breaker closing springs. (6) Apply lockout/tagout devices per a documented, established procedure. (7) Use an adequately rated test instrument to verify absence of voltage on each phase, phase-to-phase and phase-to-ground, checking the instrument on a known source before and after. (8) Where induced voltage or stored energy could be present, apply temporary protective grounds before touching the conductors.", "key_points": ["1 Identify sources → 2 interrupt load and open disconnects → 3 visually verify open", "4 Release stored electrical energy → 5 release/block stored mechanical energy", "6 Apply LOTO → 7 test for absence of voltage (live-dead-live) → 8 ground if needed", "Interrupt load current before opening a disconnect that isn't load-break rated"], "standards_reference": "NFPA 70E 120.5 (2018); NETA L2 Outline Safety B"}, {"title": "Lockout/Tagout Rules", "content": "Lockout/tagout keeps the equipment from being re-energized while you're exposed. The employer must have a documented LOTO program and procedures, and every person working on the equipment applies their own lock — one lock, one key, one person. Only the person who applied a lock removes it. If that person has left the site and can't remove it, the employer follows a specific documented procedure: verify the person isn't at the facility, make all reasonable efforts to contact them, and make sure they know the lock was removed before they return to work. A tag alone may be used only where a lock can't physically be applied, and then with an additional measure that gives equivalent safety, such as removing a fuse or opening an extra disconnect. Complex jobs (multiple crews, sources or disconnects) use a written plan, often with a group lockbox and a person in charge. Locks and tags are transferred at shift changes without leaving a gap in protection. Employees involved in LOTO are trained, retrained when procedures change, and at least every 3 years.", "key_points": ["Each worker applies a personal lock; only the person who applied it removes it", "Absent-worker lock removal requires a documented procedure and notifying the worker", "Tags alone only where a lock can't be applied, plus an additional safety measure", "LOTO retraining when procedures change, when non-compliance is found, and at least every 3 years"], "standards_reference": "NFPA 70E 120.2, 110.2(B); OSHA 29 CFR 1910.147, 1910.333(b); NETA L2 Outline Safety C"}, {"title": "Verifying Absence of Voltage — Live-Dead-Live", "content": "The test instrument must be adequately rated for the voltage and the measurement category (CAT rating) of the location. Test each phase conductor both phase-to-phase and phase-to-ground, because a phase-to-phase reading can miss a phase that's energized relative to ground. Before and after the test, prove the instrument works on a known voltage source. This is the live-dead-live method: test on a known live source, test the de-energized circuit, then test on a known live source again. Skipping the last step means a meter that failed during the test would show 'dead' on an energized circuit. On systems over 1000 V, noncontact detectors are permitted for testing each phase. A permanently mounted absence-of-voltage tester is permitted if it's listed for the purpose, installed per the manufacturer, tests every phase phase-to-phase and phase-to-ground at the point of work, and verifies itself on a known source before and after.", "key_points": ["Use an adequately rated instrument (voltage and CAT rating)", "Test every phase both phase-to-phase and phase-to-ground", "Live-dead-live: verify the meter on a known source before AND after", "Noncontact detectors are permitted only on systems over 1000 V"], "standards_reference": "NFPA 70E 120.5(7) and Exceptions 1–2, 110.2(A)(1)(e); NETA L2 Outline Safety G.2"}, {"title": "Finding Every Isolation Point", "content": "Step 1 is where most real incidents begin: a source nobody identified. Use current one-line and three-line drawings, and confirm them against the field — labels and drawings are often out of date. Look beyond the normal feeder for backfeed: standby generators and transfer switches, UPS and battery systems, solar PV and battery storage, tie breakers and double-ended substations, capacitor banks, and control power supplied from a different source. Instrument transformers can backfeed too: a potential transformer energized from its secondary steps voltage up to primary level. Other sources can induce voltage onto de-energized conductors, especially long parallel runs next to energized circuits. For each source, identify the isolation point that fully separates it — the disconnect, drawout breaker or fuse — and include it in the lockout.", "key_points": ["Verify drawings against the field; don't trust labels alone", "Check for backfeed from generators, UPS, PV/BESS, tie breakers and capacitors", "A PT energized from its secondary backfeeds full primary voltage", "Every source needs its own isolation point in the lockout"], "standards_reference": "NFPA 70E 120.5(1); NETA L2 Outline Safety G.1"}, {"title": "Temporary Protective Grounding", "content": "Temporary protective grounds protect you if the circuit is accidentally re-energized, and drain induced voltage and stored charge. They're required where induced voltage or stored energy is possible, and where conductors could contact other exposed energized parts. Grounds are applied after absence of voltage has been verified, never before. The ground set must be rated for the available fault current and the clearing time of the protective device — undersized grounds can melt and fail exactly when they're needed. Protective grounding equipment is specified by ASTM F855. The ground end is always connected first and removed last, so the conductor is never attached to a floating ground lead. On medium voltage, clamps are applied with a hot stick. Placing grounds on both sides of the work (bracketing) and bonding everything nearby creates an equipotential zone, so a fault can't put a voltage difference across your body.", "key_points": ["Apply grounds only after verifying absence of voltage", "Rate ground sets for the available fault current and clearing time (ASTM F855)", "Connect the ground end first; remove it last", "Bracket the work and bond to create an equipotential zone"], "standards_reference": "NFPA 70E 120.5(8), 120.3 (2018 numbering); ASTM F855; NETA L2 Outline Safety G.3"}], "key_values": [{"label": "Steps to establish an ESWC", "value": "8 steps, in order where feasible", "context": "Identify sources, open disconnects, visually verify, release electrical energy, release/block mechanical energy, LOTO, verify absence of voltage, ground if needed (NFPA 70E 120.5)."}, {"label": "Absence-of-voltage tests per phase", "value": "Phase-to-phase AND phase-to-ground", "context": "Both are required; a phase-to-phase reading alone can miss a phase energized relative to ground."}, {"label": "Meter verification", "value": "Before and after (live-dead-live)", "context": "Prove the instrument on a known voltage source before and after testing the circuit."}, {"label": "Noncontact voltage detectors", "value": "Permitted only over 1000 V", "context": "On systems 1000 V and below, use a contact test instrument (NFPA 70E 120.5(7) Exception 2)."}, {"label": "LOTO retraining", "value": "When procedures change, and at least every 3 years", "context": "Also when supervision or annual inspections show non-compliance (NFPA 70E 110.2(B)(2))."}, {"label": "Temporary protective ground specification", "value": "ASTM F855", "context": "Ground sets must be rated for the available fault current and clearing time."}], "exam_tips": ["If a question describes only two steps of live-dead-live and asks what's missing, the answer is re-verifying the meter on a known live source after the test.", "The person verifying absence of voltage wears full PPE for the equipment — the circuit counts as energized until verified.", "Only the person who applied a lock removes it. In an absent-worker scenario, look for the answer that includes verifying they're off site, trying to contact them, and informing them before they return.", "Grounds go on AFTER verifying absence of voltage. Any answer that grounds first is wrong.", "Noncontact 'tick tracers' are not acceptable for verifying absence of voltage at 480 V; they're only permitted over 1000 V.", "A PT backfed from its secondary is a classic exam hazard: low voltage on the secondary becomes full primary voltage."], "summary": "An electrically safe work condition takes eight ordered steps: identify every source, interrupt load and open disconnects, visually verify, release stored electrical and mechanical energy, lock and tag, verify absence of voltage, and ground where needed. Until then, the equipment is energized and the tester wears PPE. Each worker has their own lock and removes only their own. Test every phase phase-to-phase and phase-to-ground with an adequately rated meter, proven on a known source before and after. Hunt for backfeed from generators, UPS, PV, tie breakers and PTs. Apply fault-rated grounds after verification, ground end first, and bracket the work to create an equipotential zone."}$sfty$::jsonb, true, 80, 2, false
)
